{
  "subtype": "faceted_decomposition",
  "concept": "declined_expense_reasons",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "decline_reason_flags",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "This column enumerates the actual decline reasons (missing source reference, exceeds threshold, missing documentation, outside-policy department, etc.), which are literally the constitutive parts of the concept 'declined_expense_reasons'. It is an aspect OF the concept, not an external predictor."
    },
    {
      "name": "compliance_risk_level",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "Severity/risk level (Low/Medium/High) is an aspect of a decline reason -- it characterizes the compliance-risk dimension the query explicitly asks about ('compliance risks'). It decomposes the concept along its risk-severity facet rather than restating the whole."
    },
    {
      "name": "compliance_category",
      "is_constitutive_facet": true,
      "role": "facet",
      "rationale": "Grouping reasons into Documentation Gap / Authorization Control / Policy Violation is a taxonomic facet OF the decline reasons -- a higher-level aspect of the same concept, meronymically part of the reason space."
    }
  ],
  "facet_columns": ["decline_reason_flags", "compliance_risk_level", "compliance_category"],
  "non_facet_columns": [],
  "mutual_exclusivity": 0.55,
  "exhaustiveness": 0.8,
  "rationale": "All three columns are genuine facets of 'declined_expense_reasons': the specific reason flags, the risk severity, and the compliance category are each an aspect OF the concept rather than external predictors or causes. However mutual exclusivity is only moderate: decline_reason_flags already encodes the semantic content that compliance_category summarizes (e.g., 'missing_source_reference' -> Documentation Gap; 'high_value_requires_additional_approval' -> Authorization Control), so the category facet substantially overlaps with the reason facet, and risk_level is derivable from the same reason content, creating partial redundancy across facets. Exhaustiveness is fairly good: the set covers the reason itself, its risk severity, and its compliance type, which are the main constitutive aspects the query targets; a distinct 'remediation/resolution' or 'responsible-party' facet is missing but the core reason-space is well covered."
}

